        "Preferred headings moved from 42° in the natural field to 196° after reversal, a shift of 154°. Orientation under reversal was more dispersed, so the response matched the imposed rotation loosely rather than as a rigid copy. That 154° difference is the measured effect of reversal on the group's course, not a fixed room direction.",
        "Changing the horizontal component alone leaves open whether the compass reads **field polarity** or the **slope of the field lines**. Sensitivity to the manipulated geometry argues against a fixed direction in the room as the cue. Keeping the vertical component fixed isolates one geometric change at a time in this swimming preparation.",
