        "At the local inclination of 57°, turtles preferred about 90°, roughly east. At 60°, they preferred 200°, south-southwest, and at 30° the preferred heading was 57°, northeast. Fields at 75°, 45° and 15° produced no consistent group direction, and each heading was recorded in darkness. Each treatment group contained a separate set of animals.",
        "A 3° change in inclination moved the preferred heading from about 90° to about 200° while field strength stayed constant. Angle alone was enough to change the group heading, which shows discrimination between nearby inclination conditions. Field magnitude was matched in both treatments, so intensity was identical in the two groups. Each inclination produced its own group heading.",
